  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nDaily scattered shower and thunderstorms chances begin today \nwith a series of crossing shortwaves. The combination of a \nmoisture-rich airmass, above-average temperatures, and weak \nridging is expected to increase the risk for severe \nthunderstorms and heavy rainfall during the middle of the week. \nAny strong storms could produce a range of hazards, including \ndamaging wind gusts, large hail, tornadoes, and localized \nflooding. \n\nPotential for isolated to scattered severe storms has increased\nfor Wednesday afternoon and evening. Increasing surface moisture\n(widespread 70+ dew points) and warm surface temperatures will\nsignificantly boost instability by Wednesday afternoon, with\nMLCAPE values of 1500-2500+ J/kg. Bulk shear is somewhat\nmarginal, with values around 20-25kts. Still, this should be\nsufficient to support a few strong to severe storms capable of\ndamaging wind and large hail. ML guidance indicates a 15-30% \nprobability for severe weather, and SPC has introduced a\nMarginal Risk (1/5) for severe storms. \n\nML guidance has backed off severe potential a bit for Thursday,\nbut still indicates ~15% probabilities for the area. Despite \nsubstantial CAPE, weak ridging, lacking shear, and dry mid- \nlevels may inhibit updraft development. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nPotential for localized flash flooding begins this afternoon \nand evening continues for the Zanesville area as scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms overspread the region with a crossing \nshortwave. Precipitable water (PWAT) values near 2.0\" (near the \n99th percent of climatology) and dewpoints near 70F are expected\nto move into central and eastern Ohio by this afternoon. MBE \nvectors <15 kts also indicate potential for training and \nbackbuilding storms. \n\nHREF probabilities for 2 inches of rain for the ZZV area have \ndecreased a bit from previous runs (down to 20~30%), but given\nexceptionally high PWATs and potential for slow\nmoving/backbuilding storms, the threat for isolated flash\nflooding continues. WPC has highlighted the area in a Marginal\n(1/4) risk for excessive rainfall. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\nConfidence is high in temperatures of 5 to 15 \ndegrees above the climatological average today into this \nweekend. Daily high temperatures are likely to fall short of the\ndaily record highs in the mid/upper 90s this week, especially \nwith daily shower and thunderstorm chances. However, overnight \nlows in the lower 70s late week will be near record warm \nminimums. Warm overnight temperatures are particularly dangerous\nduring prolonged periods of heat. Large portions of the area \nhave a 70%+ chance of Major Heat Risk on Thursday, with high \nprobabilities of Moderate Heat Risk other days this week.",
